**The first layer of defense is exposure control.** `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include` accepts a comma-separated list of endpoint IDs. For production, expose only what you need — typically `health`, `metrics`, `info`, and `prometheus` (if you use Prometheus for monitoring).

**The next layer is Spring Security.** With `spring-boot-starter-security` on the classpath, you can restrict Actuator endpoints by role. A typical configuration secures all management endpoints behind an `ACTUATOR` role while leaving `/health` and `/info` public for load balancer checks.

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain actuatorSecurity(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .securityMatcher(EndpointRequest.toAnyEndpoint())
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            .requestMatchers(EndpointRequest.to("health", "info")).permitAll()
            .anyRequest().hasRole("ACTUATOR")
        )
        .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())
        .build();
}
```

The `EndpointRequest` matcher ensures this security configuration applies only to Actuator endpoints, keeping your main application's security rules separate. **This separation is critical** — mixing Actuator security with application security leads to accidental exposure when someone modifies the main security config.

In Spring Boot 3.x, use `@AutoConfiguration` instead of `@Configuration`. This dedicated annotation signals Boot's infrastructure that this class participates in auto-configuration ordering, and it supports `after` and `before` attributes directly.

Every auto-configuration class should be guarded by at least one condition. The minimum is `@ConditionalOnClass` — which prevents the class from activating when its required dependency isn't on the classpath. Without this guard, your auto-configuration throws `NoClassDefFoundError` in applications that don't use your library.

**The most important condition is `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`** on each `@Bean` method. This allows consumers to define their own bean and have your auto-configured one back off. Without it, consumers get `BeanDefinitionOverrideException` when they try to customize behavior.

```java
@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
@ConditionalOnClass(MetricsService.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(MetricsProperties.class)
public class MetricsAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public MetricsService metricsService(MetricsProperties props) {
        return new MetricsService(props.getEndpoint(), props.getInterval());
    }
}
```

The `@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)` ensures this class runs after the DataSource is available. Getting the ordering wrong means your bean definition references a bean that doesn't exist yet, causing a startup failure.

A subtle ordering issue appears when two auto-configuration classes have circular after/before declarations — Spring detects this and throws `IllegalStateException: @AutoConfiguration after/before cycle detected` at startup. The fix is to declare ordering only where there's a genuine dependency, not defensively.

For `@ConfigurationProperties` classes paired with auto-configuration, add `@EnableConfigurationProperties(MetricsProperties.class)` to the auto-configuration class rather than relying on `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan`. This makes the properties binding explicit and contained — consumers can see exactly which properties your auto-configuration activates without inspecting the classpath scan results.

Create the registration file at exactly `src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` with one fully-qualified class name per line. No comments, no blank lines at the end — just the class name.

```
com.example.metrics.MetricsAutoConfiguration
```

**Testing is where most tutorials stop and most production issues start.** Use `ApplicationContextRunner` to test your auto-configuration in isolation without starting a full application. It lets you simulate different classpath and property combinations.

```java
private final ApplicationContextRunner runner = new ApplicationContextRunner()
    .withConfiguration(AutoConfigurations.of(MetricsAutoConfiguration.class));

@Test
void createsServiceWhenClassPresent() {
    runner.withPropertyValues("metrics.endpoint=http://localhost:9090")
        .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx).hasSingleBean(MetricsService.class));
}

@Test
void backsOffWhenUserDefinesOwnBean() {
    runner.withBean(MetricsService.class, () -> new MetricsService("custom", 5))
        .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx.getBean(MetricsService.class)
            .getEndpoint()).isEqualTo("custom"));
}
```

The second test is critical — it proves `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` works correctly. Without this test, a consumer who defines their own `MetricsService` discovers the conflict only at their application startup, not during your library's CI. Writing this test is the contract you make with your library's consumers: "you can always override what we provide."

A third test you should always include is the negative case — verifying that the auto-configuration does nothing when required classes are absent. Use `withClassLoader(new FilteredClassLoader(MetricsService.class))` on the `ApplicationContextRunner` to simulate the class not being on the classpath. This proves `@ConditionalOnClass` is wired to the right class and that the auto-configuration truly does not activate for consumers who don't have your library.

For **IDE metadata**, add `spring-boot-configuration-processor` as an annotation processor in your autoconfigure module's `pom.xml`. It generates `META-INF/spring-configuration-metadata.json` at compile time from your `@ConfigurationProperties` class. This file powers IDE autocomplete — consumers typing `metrics.` in `application.yml` see `endpoint` and `interval` suggested with their types and your Javadoc descriptions. Without this file, consumer IDEs show yellow "unknown property" warnings for every property your library defines.

Basic configuration like `server.port`, `server.tomcat.max-threads`, and `server.ssl.*` works through properties. But some customizations require programmatic access to the server factory.

**`WebServerFactoryCustomizer<T>`** lets you configure the embedded server programmatically. The generic type determines which server you're customizing — `TomcatServletWebServerFactory`, `JettyServletWebServerFactory`, or `UndertowServletWebServerFactory`.

```java
@Bean
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> tomcatCustomizer() {
    return factory -> {
        factory.addConnectorCustomizers(connector -> {
            connector.setProperty("relaxedQueryChars", "[]");
            connector.setProperty("maxKeepAliveRequests", "200");
        });
    };
}
```

This customizer gives you access to Tomcat-specific connector properties that have no equivalent in `application.properties`. **The key advantage is that this customizer only activates when Tomcat is the actual embedded server** — if someone switches to Jetty, this bean is simply never used (assuming you guard it with `@ConditionalOnClass(Tomcat.class)`).

Beyond connector customization, `WebServerFactoryCustomizer` is also how you add custom protocol handlers, configure access logging, and adjust keep-alive behavior. For Undertow specifically, enabling HTTP/2 requires `server.http2.enabled=true` and the `undertow-core` dependency — the programmatic factory gives you access to Undertow's `Builder` API for lower-level configuration like buffer sizes and worker thread ratios that have no property-based equivalent.

A common production tuning scenario is **thread pool sizing for high-concurrency APIs**. The default `server.tomcat.threads.max=200` works for most workloads, but an API that handles many concurrent slow requests (database queries, external HTTP calls with long timeouts) exhausts this pool. Before jumping to a reactive stack, measure: add Actuator's `/metrics` and watch `tomcat.threads.busy`. If you're consistently at 90%+ utilization, either increase the thread count (up to the cost of context switching) or move the slow operations to `@Async` thread pools to free up Tomcat threads quickly. Only switch to Undertow or Netty when thread-per-request is genuinely the bottleneck — not as premature optimization.

`fixedRate = 5000` means "start every 5 seconds." If the task takes 3 seconds, you get 2 seconds of idle between runs. But if the task takes 8 seconds, **the next execution starts immediately after the previous one finishes** — on the single default thread, there's no actual overlap, but the schedule drifts. With a thread pool, overlap is possible and you need to handle concurrent access to shared state.

`fixedDelay = 5000` means "wait 5 seconds after the previous execution finishes." If the task takes 3 seconds, the gap between start times is 8 seconds (3 + 5). **This mode guarantees no overlap** because the timer doesn't start until the previous run completes. Use it when tasks should never run concurrently.

```java
@Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000)  // every 5s, may overlap with thread pool
public void pollExternalApi() { /* ... */ }

@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)  // 5s after previous finishes
public void processQueue() { /* ... */ }

@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * MON-FRI")  // 2 AM weekdays (6 fields!)
public void generateReport() { /* ... */ }
```

The cron expression uses **seconds as the first field** — `0 0 2 * * MON-FRI` is correct Spring cron, but the same expression in Unix crontab would mean something entirely different. This trips up developers coming from Linux administration.

Another mode worth knowing is `initialDelay` — the time to wait before the first execution. Without it, `fixedRate` tasks run immediately on startup. If your task queries an external service that might not be ready the moment your application starts, an `initialDelay = 30000` (30 seconds) gives the dependency time to become available before the first scheduled call.

For `@Scheduled` methods in clustered deployments (multiple instances of the same service), **every instance runs every scheduled task independently** — there is no built-in coordination. If a task must run exactly once across the cluster (e.g., a cleanup job), use ShedLock or a Quartz clustered scheduler. Without coordination, a cleanup task running on 5 instances simultaneously creates five competing database write operations, often causing lock contention and duplicate processing.

To prevent one slow task from blocking all others, configure a custom `TaskScheduler` with a thread pool.

```java
@Bean
public TaskScheduler taskScheduler() {
    ThreadPoolTaskScheduler scheduler = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
    scheduler.setPoolSize(4);
    scheduler.setThreadNamePrefix("scheduled-");
    scheduler.setErrorHandler(t -> log.error("Scheduled task failed", t));
    return scheduler;
}
```

The pool size should match the number of concurrent scheduled tasks you expect. **Setting a custom `ErrorHandler` is equally important** — by default, exceptions in scheduled tasks are silently swallowed and logged to stderr. Without an error handler, a task can fail for weeks without anyone noticing.

An alternative is `spring.task.scheduling.pool.size=4` in `application.properties`, which configures the auto-configured scheduler without writing a bean. But the programmatic approach gives you the error handler, which is the piece most production configurations need.

**Monitoring scheduled tasks** is the step most developers skip. Add Actuator and it exposes `/actuator/scheduledtasks` — a list of all registered scheduled tasks with their configuration. In production, this endpoint lets you verify that the cron expression is what you intended and that all expected tasks are registered. Combine this with Micrometer metrics to track execution time and failure counts per task.

For tasks that must not run concurrently within a single instance, add `@Async` with a single-threaded executor **and** `fixedDelay` together — `fixedRate` with a pool can still overlap if the task takes longer than the rate. `fixedDelay` with a pool of 1 guarantees sequential execution. But if you're going this far, consider whether a message queue (Kafka, SQS) with a single consumer would be a cleaner architecture than a scheduled task — queues give you persistence, retry, and visibility that cron jobs fundamentally lack.

Replace the default executor with a `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` that has explicit bounds on thread count and queue capacity.

```java
@Bean("asyncExecutor")
public Executor asyncExecutor() {
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
    executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
    executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);
    executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
    executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new CallerRunsPolicy());
    executor.setThreadNamePrefix("async-");
    return executor;
}
```

**`CallerRunsPolicy` is the safest rejection handler** — when the queue is full, the task runs on the caller's thread instead of being discarded. This provides natural backpressure: the caller slows down because it's doing the work itself, preventing the system from being overwhelmed.

Reference the executor by name with `@Async("asyncExecutor")`. Without the qualifier, Spring uses the default executor — which may still be `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` if you haven't overridden the default globally.

The **`CallerRunsPolicy` rejection handler** is worth understanding in depth. When the thread pool is at `maxPoolSize` and the queue is at `queueCapacity`, the next submitted task triggers the rejection handler. `CallerRunsPolicy` runs the task on the caller's thread — the HTTP request thread in a web application. This means the endpoint becomes synchronous under extreme load, slowing down the caller instead of dropping work. It provides natural backpressure: the caller's thread is busy doing the async work, so it can't accept more requests as fast, which propagates backpressure to the load balancer. This is almost always the right choice compared to `AbortPolicy` (throws exception, loses work) or `DiscardPolicy` (silently drops work).

For **context propagation** — passing Spring Security's `SecurityContext`, MDC logging context, or request-scoped beans across thread boundaries — `@Async` does not propagate thread-local values by default. A `@Async` method executing in the thread pool starts with a blank security context and empty MDC. Implement `TaskDecorator` and register it with the executor to copy context from the submitting thread to the worker thread.

When you have multiple runners, use `@Order` to control their execution sequence — lower values run first. Without `@Order`, the execution order is undefined and may vary between restarts.

```java
@Component
@Order(1)
public class DatabaseMigrationRunner implements CommandLineRunner {
    public void run(String... args) { /* runs first */ }
}

@Component
@Order(2)
public class CacheWarmupRunner implements ApplicationRunner {
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) { /* runs second */ }
}
```

**If any runner throws an exception, the application startup fails** — Spring publishes `ApplicationFailedEvent` instead of `ApplicationReadyEvent`, and the context is closed. This is by design: a runner failure means initialization didn't complete, so the application shouldn't accept traffic. If your runner's failure should be non-fatal, catch the exception inside the runner and log it rather than letting it propagate.

**Mapped Diagnostic Context (MDC)** attaches key-value pairs to the current thread's logging context so they appear automatically in every log line without being passed through every method. A correlation ID injected at the request entry point propagates through all log statements in that request's processing — service calls, database queries, validation errors — making it trivial to reconstruct the full request trace from aggregated logs across multiple services.

Implement MDC in a servlet filter so it wraps the entire request lifecycle.

```java
@Component
public class MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res,
                                     FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
        String correlationId = Optional.ofNullable(req.getHeader("X-Correlation-ID"))
                                       .orElse(UUID.randomUUID().toString());
        MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
        res.addHeader("X-Correlation-ID", correlationId);
        try {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        } finally {
            MDC.clear();  // always clear — thread pool reuse means stale MDC otherwise
        }
    }
}
```

The `finally` block that calls `MDC.clear()` is not optional. Tomcat's thread pool reuses threads across requests. Without clearing, a thread that processed a request for user A will have user A's MDC values when it picks up user B's request — a privacy violation hiding in your logs.

In `logback-spring.xml`, reference MDC values with `%X{correlationId}` in patterns or as custom fields in the Logstash encoder. **The production logging strategy** is: structured JSON with MDC correlation ID + `logging.level.com.yourpackage=DEBUG` in specific environments + `logging.level.root=WARN` to suppress framework noise. This combination gives you full traceability without drowning in Hibernate SQL and Spring Boot startup logs.

A well-designed `@ControllerAdvice` handles specific exception types with appropriate HTTP status codes. The key design decision is which exceptions to handle explicitly versus which to let fall through to a catch-all. Business exceptions like `ResourceNotFoundException` deserve dedicated handlers with meaningful error messages. Framework exceptions like `MethodArgumentNotValidException` need handlers that extract field-level validation errors.

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(404);
        pd.setTitle("Resource Not Found");
        pd.setDetail(ex.getMessage());
        return pd;
    }
}
```

The `ProblemDetail` object follows RFC 7807 — clients receive a consistent JSON structure regardless of which exception was thrown. The `setProperty()` method lets you add custom fields like validation errors without breaking the standard format.

**The dangerous mistake is adding a catch-all `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` that returns 400.** This hides genuine server errors — a `NullPointerException` becomes a 400 Bad Request instead of 500 Internal Server Error, and you never notice the bug. If you must have a catch-all, always return 500 for unrecognized exceptions and log the full stack trace.

When multiple handlers match an exception, **Spring selects the most specific type.** A handler for `IllegalArgumentException` takes priority over one for `RuntimeException`. A handler defined in the controller itself takes priority over one in `@ControllerAdvice` — this lets individual controllers override global behavior for specific cases.

**`@RestControllerAdvice` vs `@ControllerAdvice`** is a distinction that trips up teams building REST APIs. `@RestControllerAdvice` is a composed annotation that adds `@ResponseBody`, meaning handler methods serialize their return value as JSON automatically. With plain `@ControllerAdvice`, you must add `@ResponseBody` to each handler method or return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. For REST APIs, always use `@RestControllerAdvice`.

The most common handler you'll add beyond `ResourceNotFoundException` is one for validation errors. When a request body fails `@Valid` validation, Spring throws `MethodArgumentNotValidException`. The default behavior is a 400 with Spring's own error format. A proper handler extracts field-level errors and returns them in a structured format.

```java
@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
public ProblemDetail handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(400);
    pd.setTitle("Validation Failed");
    Map<String, String> errors = ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(FieldError::getField, FieldError::getDefaultMessage,
                                   (a, b) -> a + "; " + b));
    pd.setProperty("fieldErrors", errors);
    return pd;
}
```

To enable RFC 7807 ProblemDetail responses for Spring's own exceptions (like `HttpMessageNotReadableException`, `NoHandlerFoundException`), set `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true`. This one property standardizes all of Spring MVC's built-in error responses to the RFC 7807 format, making your API's error contract consistent without writing handlers for every framework exception. Combine it with your custom handlers that return `ProblemDetail` objects, and every error response from every source follows the same structure — a significant improvement in API client developer experience.

A `FailureAnalyzer` converts a specific exception type into a `FailureAnalysis` containing a description, action, and optional cause. Spring Boot ships analyzers for port conflicts, missing beans, and database connection failures. Writing your own takes three steps.

```java
public class MissingApiKeyAnalyzer extends AbstractFailureAnalyzer<ApiKeyMissingException> {
    @Override
    protected FailureAnalysis analyze(Throwable rootFailure, ApiKeyMissingException cause) {
        return new FailureAnalysis(
            "Required API key 'payment.gateway.key' is not configured.",
            "Set PAYMENT_GATEWAY_KEY environment variable or add payment.gateway.key to application.yml",
            cause
        );
    }
}
```

Register it in `META-INF/spring.factories` (or the Boot 3.x equivalent). When `ApiKeyMissingException` is thrown during startup, instead of a stack trace, the developer sees a formatted message with the exact property name and how to set it. **This is especially valuable for onboarding** — new team members get actionable error messages instead of debugging stack traces.

The `AbstractFailureAnalyzer<T>` generic type determines which exception type this analyzer handles. Spring's exception chain walking finds the cause of type `T` anywhere in the exception chain — not just the immediate cause. This means your analyzer fires even when the real exception is wrapped in a `BeanCreationException` several layers deep, which is the typical way Spring Boot surfaces startup failures. The `rootFailure` parameter gives you the outermost exception (usually `BeanCreationException`), and `cause` gives you the specific typed exception you declared.

For Boot 3.x, register `FailureAnalyzer` implementations in `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.diagnostics.FailureAnalyzer` (one class name per line), consistent with how auto-configurations are registered.
